The activation energy of reverse reaction will depend upon whether the forward reaction is exothermic or endothermic.
As \(\Delta \mathrm{H}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a} \text { (forward reaction) }}-\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\text { backward reaction) }}\) For exothermic reaction \(\Delta \mathrm{H}=-\mathrm{ve}\)
\(\begin{aligned}
& \therefore-\Delta \mathrm{H}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{f})}-\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{b})} \\
& \text { or } \mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{f})}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{b})}-\Delta \mathrm{H} \\
& \therefore \mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{f})}  \lt  \mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{b})}
\end{aligned}\)
for endothermic reaction
\(\begin{aligned}
& \Delta \mathrm{H}=+\mathrm{ve} \\
& \therefore \Delta \mathrm{H}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{f})}+\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{b})} \text { or } \mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{f})}=\Delta \mathrm{H}+\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{b})} \\
& \therefore \mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{f})}  \gt  \mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{a}(\mathrm{b})} \text {. }
\end{aligned}\)

Put \(x=\sin ^2 \theta\) and \(a=\sin ^2 \phi\). then
\(\begin{aligned}
& y=\sin ^{-1}(\sin \theta \cos \phi-\cos \theta \sin \phi) \\
& =\sin ^{-1} \sqrt{x}-\sin ^{-1} \sqrt{a} \\
& \therefore \quad \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x}} \cdot \frac{1}{2 \sqrt{x}}
\end{aligned}\)

Given that \(\mathrm{a} * \mathrm{~b}=\frac{\mathrm{ab}}{4} \forall \mathrm{a}, \mathrm{b} \in \mathrm{Q}^{+}\)
\(\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad 3 *\left(\frac{1}{5} * \frac{1}{2}\right) & =3 *\left\{\frac{\frac{1}{5} \times \frac{1}{2}}{4}\right\}=3 * \frac{1}{40}=\frac{\left(3 \times \frac{1}{40}\right)}{4} \\
& =\frac{3}{160}
\end{aligned}\)

Using formula
\(\begin{aligned}
& 2 \mathrm{~d} \sin \theta=\mathrm{n} \lambda \\
& \mathrm{d}=\frac{\mathrm{n} \lambda}{2 \sin \theta}=\frac{1 \times 20 \times 10^{-3}}{2 \sin 30^{\circ}}
\end{aligned}\)
\(\mathrm{d}=\frac{20 \times 10^{-3}}{2 \times \frac{1}{2}}=20 \mathrm{~nm}\)

We know that \(\Delta \mathrm{U}=-\mathrm{W}\) Therefore
\(\begin{aligned}
& \Delta \mathrm{U}=-\int_0^{\mathrm{x}} \mathrm{Fdx} \text { or } \Delta \mathrm{U}=-\int_0^{\mathrm{x}} \mathrm{kxdx} \\
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{U}_{(\mathrm{x})}-\mathrm{U}_{(0)}=-\frac{\mathrm{kx}^2}{2} \text { given } \mathrm{U}_{(0)}=0, \\
& \mathrm{U}_{(\mathrm{x})}=-\frac{\mathrm{kx}^2}{2}
\end{aligned}\)
